WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.030
A student’s attendance app

2
00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:04.150
checked each name against a list

3
00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:05.870
of twenty thousand students.

4
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.980
Every check walked the whole

5
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.260
list, and the app crawled.

6
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.740
He changed one line, from a list to

7
00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.680
a set, and it became instant.

8
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.600
Today: data structures, and how

9
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.120
to choose one by the job.

10
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.200
Choose a structure by what

11
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.260
the job does most.

12
00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:30.020
A list keeps items in order, by position.

13
00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.990
A stack takes from the top; a

14
00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:33.950
queue from the front.

15
00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.750
A set answers one question

16
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.750
fast: is it there?

17
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.320
And a dictionary finds a value by its key.

18
00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.150
A stack is last in, first out,

19
00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:47.690
like a pile of plates.

20
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.600
Push adds to the top, and

21
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.060
pop takes from the top.

22
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.750
The back button, undo and the

23
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.430
call stack all work this way.

24
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.180
In Python, a list’s append

25
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.780
and pop make a stack.

26
00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.570
A queue is first in, first out, like

27
00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.290
the canteen line or the printer.

28
00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.040
Items join at the back and

29
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:10.900
leave from the front.

30
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.300
Taking from the front of a big list is

31
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.340
slow, because every item shifts along.

32
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.100
For big queues, Python has a deque.

33
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.600
Here’s a classic job for a

34
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.180
stack: checking brackets.

35
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.040
Each opening bracket is pushed.

36
00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.960
Each closing one pops the top,

37
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:33.660
which must be its partner.

38
00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.830
If every pair matches and the stack

39
00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.890
ends empty, the brackets balance.

40
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:40.680
Quick check!

41
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.620
Your notes app keeps an undo history: the

42
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.060
last change made is the first one undone.

43
00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:48.630
Which structure fits?

44
00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.850
A stack: last in, first out.

45
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.860
Each change is pushed, and

46
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.400
undo pops the newest.

47
00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.270
A queue would undo your oldest

48
00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.730
change first, which nobody wants.

49
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.420
Sets and dictionaries jump straight to

50
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.420
where an item would be, using a hash.

51
00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.510
So checking whether x is in a set takes

52
00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.890
about one step, however big it grows.

53
00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.550
A list checks its items one by one.

54
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.800
The price: a set keeps no

55
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.760
order and no repeats.

56
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.100
A dictionary finds a value by its key.

57
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.380
To count votes, use each candidate as a

58
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.100
key, and add one each time it appears.

59
00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:38.050
Get, with a default of zero, handles a

60
00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.750
candidate seen for the first time.

61
00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.740
One pass, and every count is ready.

62
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:47.000
In the app, open Stacks and queues.

63
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.250
Push and pop the undo stack of a notes

64
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.490
app, then serve a queue at the bank.

65
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.740
Before each step, predict

66
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:55.800
what comes out next.

67
00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:57.850
Your turn.

68
00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.090
Your program checks, thousands of times,

69
00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.570
whether a student ID is registered.

70
00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.230
Which structure should hold

71
00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.350
the registered IDs?

72
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.240
A set: about one step per check.

73
00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.170
In short: choose a structure by

74
00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.870
the operations the job needs.

75
00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.230
Stacks are last in, first out;

76
00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.190
queues first in, first out.

77
00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.050
Sets answer is it there, and

78
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.130
dictionaries find values by key.

79
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.440
Try it yourself: write the

80
00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:31.880
bracket checker in full.

81
00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.360
Push each opening bracket, and on each

82
00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.740
closing one, pop and check it’s the

83
00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:37.960
partner.

84
00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.360
Test it on an empty string, a mismatched

85
00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.700
pair, and a string that only opens.
